// Caper: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== caper.f01 | feature | three-act
title: Four Empty Hooks
genre: caper + comedy
tone: Light-fingered, deadpan and very Parisian
era: 1911 | period
setting: A great national museum and the lodging houses around it, Paris | fictional | city
logline: A glazier who once fitted the glass on a famous portrait walks out of the museum with it under his smock on a Monday, and then discovers that nobody notices for a day, nobody suspects him for two years, and the man who put him up to it never meant to collect it.
premise: The museum closes on Mondays for cleaning. Workmen in white smocks are invisible. {vittorio} is a house-painter and glazier, homesick and often insulted, who believes the picture was looted from his country and ought to go home. A plausible gentleman, {the_marquis}, has encouraged him in this belief and asked only to be told when it is done.
synopsis: The first act is the easiest theft in history. {vittorio} hides in a cupboard on Sunday, lifts the panel off four hooks on Monday morning, takes it out of its frame on a service stair, cannot open the door at the bottom, and is let out by a helpful plumber. The gap on the wall is assumed to be the photographers'. It is Tuesday noon before a visiting painter asks a guard where the lady is. The second act is the uproar and the wait. The museum shuts for a week. Crowds queue to look at the empty hooks. The police measure the staff's thumbs and question a poet and a painter of the avant-garde, who are terrified for reasons of their own. Inspector {garnier} interviews {vittorio} in his room and uses, as a writing desk, the trunk the panel is in. {vittorio} waits for {the_marquis} to send for the picture. No word comes. What the gentleman wanted was the theft, not the object: with the original publicly missing, his forger {lucien} can sell six "originals" to six millionaires who can never compare notes or complain. {vittorio} lives for two years with the most famous face in the world under his stove, talks to her, and grows fond and resentful. His landlady {madame_seguin} thinks he has a woman up there. In the third act he takes the panel home across the border in the false bottom of the trunk and offers it to a dealer for his country's museums, with a request for expenses. He is arrested in his hotel, baffled. At the trial he is a patriot to half the room and a simpleton to the rest; he gets seven months. The picture goes back on its hooks behind new glass, three times as famous as before. {the_marquis} is never charged with anything, there being no law against selling a rich man a picture and letting him think what he likes. In the last scene {vittorio}, released, buys a postcard of her.
question: Who actually stole what?
themes: fame made by absence; the invisible workman; patriotism as a mark's weakness
audience: General; lovers of dry caper comedy
viewpoint: With {vittorio}, who is always the last to understand his own crime
stakes: Prison, a national treasure, and a small man's dignity
conflict: A simple man's simple theft against a confidence trick so elegant he never sees it
ending: He returns the picture himself and is jailed briefly. The swindler keeps the money. The painting is more famous than it ever was.
experience: Giggles at institutional blindness, two years of tender absurdity with a masterpiece under a stove, and a wry last look
look: White smocks in long galleries; four hooks on a damask wall; a garret with a trunk; crowds staring at nothing
sound: A museum's echo, a cafe piano, a newsboy shouting. The scrape of a trunk pulled from under a bed.
m: restore | secret | belonging-vs-identity | ironic
@ vittorio | Vittorio Perani | lead | protagonist | human | he/him | 30 | To take her home and be thanked | He is called names in the workshops of a city that is not his | He has been aimed like a tool | Proud, literal, lonely | From thief to custodian to bewildered patriot | Accented French, short sentences | White smock, moustache, glazier's putty knife
@ the_marquis | The Marquis | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 48 | Six sales and no stolen goods | It is the perfect crime because he commits none | He must never touch the picture | Charming, unhurried, immaculate | Unchanged, and uncharged | Silky, international | Grey gloves, a cane, a card with a title on it
@ lucien | Lucien Marchand | supporting | foil | human | he/him | 55 | To be paid for the best work of his life | He is a restorer nobody credits | Six copies that can never be shown | Meticulous, melancholy, proud of his crackle | From craftsman to a man with six secrets | Murmurs to his brushes | Stained cuffs, a loupe on a ribbon
@ garnier | Inspector Garnier | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 52 | An arrest before the minister falls | The whole world is laughing at Paris | He wrote his notes on the trunk | Pompous, thorough in the wrong places | From certainty to a very quiet retirement | Official, rolling | Top hat, a waxed moustache, a tape for thumbs
@ madame_seguin | Madame Seguin | featured | ally | human | she/her | 60 | Her rent, and a little romance by proxy | She likes the quiet Italian | She is sure there is a woman in his room | Nosy, kind, wrong | From suspicion to tearful testimony | Gossip at speed | Apron, keys, curl papers
~ vittorio - the_marquis | workman and gentleman | flattered into a mission | never sent for
~ vittorio - garnier | suspect and inspector | a routine interview on a trunk | a dock and a red face
~ the_marquis - lucien | swindler and forger | a commission for six | paid, and sworn to silence by his own vanity
# Monday | INT | Museum, Salon | DAY | vittorio | Lift her down | Closed for cleaning. A white smock. Four hooks. The panel is smaller than he remembered | The portrait is taken | 5 | props=portrait panel+frame
# Monday | INT | Museum, Service Stair | DAY | vittorio | Get out | The frame left on the stairs. A locked door. A doorknob in his pocket. A plumber obliges with pliers and wishes him good day | | 4 | props=doorknob
# Monday | INT | Museum, Salon | DAY | garnier, madame_seguin | Notice | Tuesday noon. A painter asks a guard where she is. The guard says: at the photographers'. She is not at the photographers' | The theft is discovered a day late | 5 | crowd=40
# The Empty Wall | EXT | Museum Courtyard | DAY | garnier, the_marquis | Queue for nothing | Thousands come to look at four hooks. Hawkers sell postcards. {the_marquis} buys one and tips his hat to the inspector | | 4 | crowd=400
# The Empty Wall | INT | Lodging House, Garret | DAY | vittorio, garnier, madame_seguin | Be interviewed | The inspector takes a statement, writing on the lid of the trunk. {madame_seguin} hovers. He signs it on the trunk too | | 6 | props=trunk
# The Empty Wall | INT | Forger's Studio | NIGHT | the_marquis, lucien | Sell her six times | Six panels drying, each cracked by hand. Six buyers, six oceans apart, none of whom can ever ask. The original is not required | The real scheme is shown | 5 | props=six copies
# The Empty Wall | INT | Lodging House, Garret | NIGHT | vittorio | Keep her | Two winters. He props her on a chair and eats his soup. He tells her nobody has written | | 4 | props=portrait panel+stove
# Home | INT | Hotel Room, Florence | DAY | vittorio, garnier | Give her back | A dealer, an expert, a request for expenses. Policemen. He asks what he has done wrong | He is arrested returning it | 5 | props=trunk with false bottom
# Home | INT | Museum, Salon | DAY | vittorio, madame_seguin | Buy a postcard | New glass, a rope, a crowd ten deep. Seven months later a workman pays two sous for her picture and puts it in his hat | | 3 | crowd=200
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from a museum theft of 1911 and the legend of the copies, with invented people and an unnamed picture
